# Ansible-managed. Do not edit by hand.
#
# SHARED BY EVERY HOST THAT RESOLVES THROUGH PI-HOLE — rendered by roles/setup/k3s
# (daniel-box) and roles/setup/optimize_pi (daniel-pi), each passing its own
# `common_resolver_nameservers`. It lives in setup/common rather than in either caller
# because what drifts is the REASONING below, not the four lines of config at the bottom.
# Templates resolve against the calling role's own templates/ directory, so both callers
# name this file by absolute path.
#
# This host resolves through the in-cluster Pi-hole, with a public resolver behind it, and
# switches between them per query in BOTH directions with no daemon watching anything.
#
# WHY A STATIC FILE RATHER THAN A systemd-resolved DROP-IN. resolved is stateful and sticky.
# Its own man page: it "will continuously talk to the same server for all queries on a
# particular lookup scope until some form of error is seen at which point it switches to the
# next, and then continuously stays with it ... eventually returning to the first configured
# server", and it warns that "listed DNS servers per lookup scope must be equivalent in the
# zones they serve". Pi-hole and Cloudflare are NOT equivalent — one blocks and answers the
# local zone, the other does neither — so a resolved DNS= list yields a coin flip rather than
# a preference. Measured on daniel-pi 2026-09-01, 45 minutes after exactly that config was
# applied by PR #693: `Current DNS Server` read 1.1.1.1 at 15:12 and 10.0.0.243 at 15:14,
# unprompted. PR #697 replaced it with this file.
#
# glibc has no such state. resolv.conf(5): "the resolver library queries them in the order
# listed", and the `rotate` option — which this file deliberately does NOT set — is documented
# as spreading load "rather than having all clients try the first listed server first every
# time". Not setting it is what makes the first server the preferred one on EVERY query. So:
#   - cluster down at boot -> Pi-hole times out, the query falls through, the host resolves
#   - Pi-hole comes up     -> the very next query tries it first again, with no probe or reload
#   - Pi-hole dies later   -> the next query falls through again
# Automatic in both directions, by construction rather than by health check.
#
# THE COST is {{ common_resolver_options }}: `attempts` counts rounds over the WHOLE list, not
# retries per server, so one round is Pi-hole (up to {{ common_resolver_timeout }}s) then the
# fallback, then an error. While Pi-hole is down every lookup on this host pays that timeout
# once, and a single dropped UDP packet falls through with no retry. Disabling resolved also
# gives up its DNS cache — nothing else on these hosts caches, so every lookup goes over the
# wire. That is the trade against running a health-checking forwarder here.
#
# daniel-server has run exactly this file since 2026-08-10 and is the in-fleet evidence that
# the end state is stable, on a k3s node and with Docker containers forwarding to it.
{% for server in common_resolver_nameservers %}
nameserver {{ server }}
{% endfor %}
options {{ common_resolver_options }}
